Introduction                                                                                                         

This document collects the scientific literature regarding cancer.                                                   


Spatial Transcriptomics and Pan-Cancer Metastasis                                                                    

Recent advances in spatially resolved single-cell transcriptomics have provided unprecedented insights into the tumor
microenvironment (TME) and metastatic processes. Large-scale atlases of pan-cancer brain metastases have been        
constructed, revealing complex cellular interactions and spatial organization within the metastatic niche            
[^10.5281_zenodo.21782884][^10.5281_zenodo.21782885][^10.5281_zenodo.21816284][^10.5281_zenodo.21816285].            
Furthermore, pan-cancer co-expression network analyses have identified hub genes that not only define tumor states   
but also show strong potential for predicting patient responses to targeted therapies [^10.5281_zenodo.2574643].     


Metabolic Reprogramming and Nutritional Interventions                                                                

Cancer cells exhibit profound metabolic plasticity, which can be exploited through dietary and nutritional           
strategies. Omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids, particularly eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA), have been shown to induce 
mitochondrial succinylation, disrupting metabolic homeostasis and triggering ferroptosis in prostate cancer cells    
[^10.17638_03195029]. Similarly, cyclic fasting and fasting-mimicking diets elevate systemic polyunsaturated fatty   
acids, which cooperate with nutrient starvation to induce lipid peroxidation and ferroptosis, demonstrating          
significant antitumor efficacy in breast cancer models [^10.21954_ou.ro.00108036].                                   


Oncogenic Signaling and Chromatin Regulation                                                                         

Dysregulation of key signaling pathways and chromatin architecture remains a central driver of tumorigenesis.        
Deubiquitinating enzymes (DUBs) have emerged as critical regulators of the Hippo pathway, with USP42 identified as a 
key modulator of YAP/TAZ activity in pleural mesothelioma [^10.17638_03193849]. Concurrently, the DEK oncoprotein has
been characterized as a dynamic chromatin architectural factor that drives transcriptional reprogramming and         
metastatic progression in melanoma, with its activity heavily dependent on CK2-mediated phosphorylation              
[^10.17638_03194520].                                                                                                


Liquid Biopsy and Non-Invasive Diagnostics                                                                           

The shift towards non-invasive diagnostics has accelerated the development of liquid biopsy techniques. Cell-free DNA
(cfDNA) profiling, particularly focusing on nucleosome positioning and repetitive genomic elements, has proven highly
effective in distinguishing cancer patients from healthy controls across multiple tumor types, including glioblastoma
and colorectal cancer [^10.5526_err-00042862][^10.5526_err-00042900]. Additionally, comprehensive RNA-seq analyses in
specific cohorts have mapped differential gene expression landscapes in colorectal cancer, highlighting cell cycle   
and IL-17 pathways as potential therapeutic targets [^10.5281_zenodo.8307739]. Urinary proteomics is also being      
leveraged to identify novel biomarkers for early breast cancer detection [^10.5281_zenodo.20393677].                 


Multi-Omics Prognostics and Therapeutic Development                                                                  

Integrating multi-omic data is revolutionizing prognostic modeling and drug discovery. Computational frameworks      
analyzing over 10,000 checkpoint blockade treatment cases have successfully dissected tumor-immune ecosystems,       
identifying prognostic cellular programs across diverse malignancies                                                 
[^10.5281_zenodo.18706180][^10.5281_zenodo.18706179]. In parallel, the development of non-genotoxic topoisomerase II 
catalytic inhibitors represents a promising avenue for novel cancer therapeutics that avoid traditional genotoxic    
side effects [^10.14288_1.0451818].                                                                                  


Environmental and Epidemiological Factors                                                                            

Beyond molecular mechanisms, external factors significantly influence cancer etiology. Research continues to         
elucidate how the built environment correlates with cancer risk, emphasizing the role of urban planning and          
environmental exposures in oncological outcomes [^10.14288_1.0451801].                                               


Renal Cell Carcinoma Metastasis Microenvironment                                                                     

Specific tumor types exhibit unique microenvironmental adaptations that drive progression. Recent spatial multi-omics
studies on clear cell renal cell carcinoma (ccRCC) lung metastases have revealed the formation of confined tumor     
microenvironments characterized by cancer-associated fibroblast lymphocyte exclusion sites, highlighting new targets 
for overcoming immune evasion [^10.5281_zenodo.18767278][^10.5281_zenodo.18767279].                                  

